  "query_preview": "..\n\nSoutherly flow downstream of the plains troughing will bring one \nmore day of above normal warmth that will approach/reach daily \nrecord high temperatures for today. The ability to reach daily highs \nwill depend on the degree of cloud cover that is expected to arrive \nahead of the inbound height falls. Timing of clouds is forecast to \narrive late morning/early afternoon with thicker cloud coverage by \nmid to late afternoon. This should keep high temperatures across much\nof the forecast area in the low-mid 60s before rain chances increase.\nThe inbound trough will drive a cold front through the region with \nenough improvement in moisture quality with sufficient frontogenesis \nto support light rain showers while better forcing/dynamics is \npositioned off to the north. Timing of rainfall will be mainly \nbetween 12 pm and 9 pm this as the southwest to northeast oriented \nrainfall axis slides through southeast Michigan. \n  \nThe passing of the cold front will mark the end of the well above \nnormal temperature stretch and take a turn to much colder conditions \nstarting Monday after the better cold advection kicks in tonight. \nTemperatures at 850mb will fall towards -10C by Monday evening \nresulting in high temperatures down into the low-mid 40s to start \nthe work week. A couple of interacting Canadian mid-level waves will \nhelp drive this colder air and bring a period of increased dynamics \nthat will take advantage of an already active lake moisture flux to \nbring increased chances for flurries and light snow showers. Greatest\nchances for light snow showers still looks to be along and north of \nI-69, but would expect flurry potential to be widespread come Monday \nlate afternoon/evening as the wave directly overhead. \n\nMore stable conditions will follow the Monday wave as high pressure \nslides through the region Tuesday into Wednesday bringing dry. After\na cold start to Tuesday morning with low temperatures in the low-mid\n20s, Tuesday's high temperatures are currently forecast to be the \ncoldest of the week with most areas peaking in the upper 30s. Much of\nWednesday is likely to stay on the dry side with a slight bump in \ndaytime highs into the 40s as the colder thermal trough pushes east. \nThere is increasing precipitation potential for Thursday as long \nrange models/ensembles point toward a developing closed low with \nsoutheast Michigan on the northwest west flank of the associated \nsurface reflection. More details to be hashed out closer to the event\nas precipitation type will depend lower level thermal profiles and \nhow cool enough it may be for snow p-type. At this time, it looks \nlike rain as the more dominant p-type with a chance for snow to mix \nin. Temperatures to close out the week will be around the normal for \nmid-late November.",
